<rss version="0.92"><channel><item>
            <title>[LU-20997] llite: PCC read fallback uses IOCB_DIRECT with backend aops after mmap</title>
                <link>https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-20997</link>
                <project id="10000" key="LU">Lustre</project>
                    <description>&lt;p&gt;After a PCC file is mmapped, pcc_mmap_mapping_set() points the Lustre inode mapping a_ops at the PCC backend (e.g. ext4) aops, until detach. On kernels v6.10+ those aops have no &lt;del&gt;&amp;gt;direct_IO. If a PCC-RO read then fails with an error pcc_io_tolerate() does not tolerate (any backend -EIO, or OBD_FAIL_LLITE_PCC_FAKE_ERROR), do_file_read_iter() falls back to the Lustre read path with IOCB_DIRECT still set: ll_file_io_generic()&lt;/del&gt;&amp;gt;vvp_io_read_start()&lt;del&gt;&amp;gt;generic_file_read_iter() then calls mapping&lt;/del&gt;&amp;gt;a_ops-&amp;gt;direct_IO(), which is NULL in the backend aops.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It can be hit by a file opened O_DIRECT before mmap, or by hybrid IO (large buffered reads get IOCB_DIRECT, on by default). Change 67003 (&lt;a href="https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-20388" title="Ubuntu 26.04 sanity-pcc test_2b: FAIL: /mnt/lustre/d2b.sanity-pcc/multiop expected pcc state: none, but got: readwrite" class="issue-link" data-issue-key="LU-20388"&gt;LU-20388&lt;/a&gt;, PCC mmap fix for mmap_prepare) additionally sets FMODE_CAN_ODIRECT unconditionally in ll_file_open(), which lets explicit O_DIRECT opens succeed in this state on newer kernels.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Suggested fix: keep IOCB_DIRECT off the Lustre fallback (or skip the fallback) while inode-&amp;gt;i_mapping-&amp;gt;a_ops != &amp;amp;ll_aops, with a regression test. Found reviewing &lt;a href="https://review.whamcloud.com/c/fs/lustre-release/+/67003" class="external-link" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener"&gt;https://review.whamcloud.com/c/fs/lustre-release/+/67003&lt;/a&gt; (AI review comment on lustre/llite/file.c); Oleg Drokin asked for a separate ticket and a separate patch.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
